  <iati-identifier>US-GOV-1-617-IL-2020-MRRH</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="US-GOV-1" type="10">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">U.S. Agency for International Development</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mbarara Regional Referral Hospital (MRHH) Strengthening Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Uganda):  MRRH is a government-to-government award that supports Uganda&apos;s efforts to bridge the gap between the central and district levels of the health system. MRRH will enhance oversight and coordination functions of the Ministry of Health to maximize effectiveness and efficiency. With FY 2022 nutrition, MCH, and TB funds, the activity will leverage PEPFAR support to build a regional platform for service delivery improvement and capacity building in lower-level health facilities. TB funding will strengthen drug resistance case management in the regional referral hospitals and improve the TB treatment success rate to 90%, as envisioned in the FY 2022 TB roadmap. Nutrition, MCH and TB funding will contribute to cross-cutting Health Systems Support.MRRH will prioritize improvement initiatives in the region and serve as a center of excellence for nutrition, TB, and maternal/neonatal/adolescent/child health services in the 11 districts in the Ankole region, while ensuring the reduction of gender-based violence and the promotion of gender equality. MRRH will act as a regional hub to coordinate capacity building in the region, and as a central platform for sustainable skills transfer to the lower level health facilities.MRRH will prioritize improving regional hospital-district patient referral and information flow; data-driven decision making; knowledge management; standardization of supportive supervision and policy dissemination; quality assurance; and district- and regional-level planning and coordination. MRRH will fund activities to: 1) improve gender equality and empower women to access and use high quality health services; 2) prevent gender-based violence and address the impact of gender-based violence; 3) improve nutrition outcomes; and 4) build the capacity of health facilities to strengthen outreach and mobile clinics to ensure continuity of services during climate-related disruptions..</narrative>
  </description>
